The wind was a hungry thing, gnawing at the stones of the Point Reyes lighthouse. Elias, his beard a frosted thicket, made his nightly rounds, the beam of his lamp cutting a path through the swirling dark. He was a man of routines, his life measured in the steady, rhythmic pulse of the great lens above him. Solitude was his companion, and the sea his only conversation.

It was on the second Tuesday of November that the sea offered a reply.

He found it wedged between two rocks at the base of the cliff, a slick, green-glass bottle. The cork was sealed with black wax, and inside, a single sheet of paper, curled like a sleeping fern. He carried it up to the lamp room, the warmth of the great light a stark contrast to the biting cold outside. He uncorked it with a grunt, the seal breaking with a satisfying pop.

The paper was dry, the ink a faded, watery blue. The handwriting was elegant, looping, and feminine.

*To the Keeper of the Light,*

*My name is Clara. I am writing this on the eve of my wedding to a man I do not love. He is a merchant, wealthy and kind, but his eyes are the color of shallow water. The man I love is a fisherman, and his eyes are the color of the deep sea, where all the secrets live. Tomorrow, I will say vows I do not mean. Tonight, I
